WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.120
Usually Polymorph is used to
replace layers in your comp, but

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.800
we can actually use this to
attach layers to Target layers.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.179
So here, we've got three Crate layers,

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.279
each of them animated

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:13.759
differently.

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:16.019
So Crate 1 is animated only with

00:00:16.019 --> 00:00:19.359
keyframes, Crate 2 is animated

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:21.760
with a looping wiggle expression

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.440
on the Position, Scale, and

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.019
Rotation, and Crate 3 has

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:28.600
keyframes on Position and

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.839
Rotation, and both of those have

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:32.619
a loopOut("pingpong")

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.740
expression – so, a mix of

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.560
keyframed and expression animation.

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:43.920
We have this Cat layer here that
has an expression applied to the

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.560
Y Position and the Rotation to
make it do a little dance.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.079
So if I play this through now,
you can see that the crates are

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:53.719
all moving differently.

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:54.960
The cat is dancing.

00:00:56.439 --> 00:00:59.719
We want to place a cat in each
of the crates.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.769
So here with Polymorph, there's

00:01:05.769 --> 00:01:06.810
a few settings that we'll need

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.609
to set in order to be able to do

00:01:08.609 --> 00:01:09.930
that.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.930
Layer order: We want to make
sure that the new layer is

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.650
placed below the Target layer.

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:18.849
Layer  parent: Yes, we want to
make sure that the new layer is

00:01:18.849 --> 00:01:22.650
parented to the Target layer.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.849
Visibility: We want to show the
Target layer.

00:01:25.849 --> 00:01:27.090
So the Targets are each of these

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.829
crates – we don't want to hide
them,

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:29.890
and we don't want to delete

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:33.370
them. Expressions:

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.290
Now as I've said, there are
expressions applied to some

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.329
of the crates. And

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:42.409
there's an expression – or there
are expressions – applied to the
cat.

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:43.329
We don't want

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:45.650
the crate expressions to be

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.209
applied to the cat. We want

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:47.329
the

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:50.090
cats to keep whatever
expressions are

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.450
currently in place,

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.870
so here we want to keep

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.170
Source expressions only. If I

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:55.450
apply

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.409
that now… We don't need to worry
about Track Matte here and we

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:02.810
don't need to worry about
Separate Dimensions. Delete
Source layer:

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.170
Yeah, we don't need the original
cat at the end, so

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:09.020
I'm just going to click on Yes
and apply. If I open

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.740
my settings here quickly, you
can see that our Source layer is

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.120
set to be the last selected
layer. So I'm going

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.740
to select the three Target
layers first – so these are our
three

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.960
crates. And then also

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.620
our cat – so the cat is the last
selected layer; that is going to

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.780
be our Source. If I come over

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.259
here now and click on Polymorph,
straight

00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:32.439
away you can see

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:35.300
that there has been a cat
created

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.219
for each of the crates. If I

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:37.580
play this

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:43.560
now, you can see that the cats
are attached. Now, because the
cats

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.020
are – or the source layers,
rather – are positioned

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.620
to wherever the anchor point of

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.259
the Target layer is,

00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:50.699
the cats are kind of hidden

00:02:50.699 --> 00:02:51.680
a little bit, so I'm just going

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.620
to open up the Position
properties and

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.860
drag that up a little.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.240
And if I come back to

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:58.379
the

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:03.860
start, you can see now that we
have those three crates, each of
those crates

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.460
has a cat attached to it, and we
still have the

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.280
cat dancing, keeping its
animation, and the crates are
keeping all of

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.139
their respective animations.
